{"name":"Amphitrite","hex":"#384e47","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Amphitrite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Terebellidae – spaghetti worms, sea-floor-dwelling polychetes."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A nymph, the wife of Poseidon.","29 Amphitrite, a main belt asteroid."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Amphitrite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amphitrite","blocks":[{"text":"In ancient Greek mythology, Amphitrite was the goddess of the sea, the queen of the sea, and her consort is Poseidon. She was a daughter of Nereus and Doris. Under the influence of the Olympian pantheon, she became the consort of Poseidon and was later used as a symbolic representation of the sea. Her Roman counterpart is Salacia, a comparatively minor figure, and the goddess of saltwater."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Amphitrite_Penteskouphia_Louvre_MNC208.jpg/330px-Amphitrite_Penteskouphia_Louvre_MNC208.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphitrite","wikidata":"Q180222"},{"title":"Amphitrite (1802 ship)","blocks":[{"text":"Amphitrite was built at Appledore, Torridge, and launched in 1802. Under various owners and masters she traded across the North Atlantic and to the Baltic. She was wrecked in 1833 with heavy loss of life while transporting female convicts to New South Wales."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Naufrage_de_l%27Amphitrite_le_31_ao%C3%BBt_1833-gravure.jpg/330px-Naufrage_de_l%27Amphitrite_le_31_ao%C3%BBt_1833-gravure.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphitrite_(1802_ship)","wikidata":"Q2141987"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Theseus_Athena_Amphitrite_Louvre_G104.jpg/250px-Theseus_Athena_Amphitrite_Louvre_G